Wrote `/app/polyglot/main.py.c` — a single-file polyglot that is valid Python 3 and valid C.

**How it works:** the opening `#include <stdio.h>` line is a Python comment (`#`) and a C preprocessor directive; the entire Python implementation ends in `sys.exit(...)`, so Python stops there. That code is wrapped in `r'''…'''` — a C raw string literal (a no-op expression) whose closing `;` ends it — so the C compiler skips the Python and sees only the C `main`. The trailing `/*''';#*/` comment is a C block comment while Python reads an unused string expression.

